# Architecture-First Development Framework (AFDF)

**Version:** 2.0  
**Author:** Dr. Ephraim Mpofu

---

# Purpose

The Architecture-First Development Framework (AFDF) is a universal methodology for designing and building enterprise software, AI systems, multi-agent platforms, automation solutions, and digital products.

It is based on one fundamental principle:

> **Knowledge defines Architecture. Architecture defines Implementation. Implementation realizes the Architecture.**

The architecture is the **Single Source of Truth** for development, while the knowledge foundation is the **Primary Source of Truth** for the architecture itself.

---

# Core Principles

## 1. Knowledge Before Architecture

Architecture must be derived from authoritative knowledge rather than assumptions.

Examples include:

- TOGAF
- BABOK
- PMBOK
- COBIT
- ITIL
- ISO Standards
- Academic Research
- Internal Company Standards
- Industry Regulations
- Subject Matter Experts

---

## 2. Architecture Before Implementation

No production code is written until the architecture has been approved.

---

## 3. Single Source of Truth

Every implementation must trace back to approved architecture.

Every architectural decision must trace back to an approved knowledge source.

---

## 4. Separation of Concerns

Knowledge defines architecture.

Architecture defines implementation.

Implementation builds.

Testing verifies.

Governance approves.

Each concern remains independent.

---

## 5. Incremental Development

Development proceeds one Work Package at a time.

Each Work Package is:

- implemented
- tested
- validated
- committed
- approved

before the next begins.

---

## 6. Continuous Architectural Compliance

Every implementation is continuously validated against the approved architecture.

No implementation may violate architectural decisions.

