# Runtime Knowledge Lifecycle

After the platform has been built, users provide enterprise-specific knowledge that the platform processes.

Examples include:

- Company Policies
- Business Strategies
- Process Documentation
- Organization Charts
- Architecture Documents
- Requirements
- Regulations
- Contracts
- PDFs
- Word Documents
- Excel Files
- PowerPoint Presentations

This knowledge **does not define the platform**; it enables the platform to reason about a specific enterprise.

Runtime processing follows:

```
Enterprise Documents

↓

Document Intelligence

↓

OCR (if required)

↓

Text Extraction

↓

Semantic Chunking

↓

Entity Extraction

↓

TOGAF Mapping

↓

Knowledge Normalization

↓

Enterprise Knowledge Layer

↓

Enterprise Intelligence Framework

↓

Enterprise Repository
```

---

# Work Package Methodology

Every implementation is delivered as an independent Work Package.

Example

```
WP1

Repository Extension

↓

Implementation

↓

Testing

↓

Architecture Compliance

↓

Git Commit

↓

Approval
```

Only after approval does development proceed to the next Work Package.

---

# Work Package Requirements

Every Work Package must contain:

## Summary

Description of implemented functionality.

---

## Files

- Files created
- Files modified

---

## Tests

- Unit Tests
- Integration Tests
- System Tests (where applicable)

---

## Architecture Compliance

Evidence that implementation follows the approved architecture.

---

## Dependencies

Required Work Packages.

Required architectural deliverables.

---

## Risks

Known limitations.

Future improvements.

---

## Git

Commit all completed work before beginning the next Work Package.

---

